Task 2: Design and Build a Reusable Terraform Module Library
Problem Statement
Design and implement a local Terraform module library containing at least two modules â one for network topology (VPC, subnets, route tables) and one for compute resources (security group, EC2 instances via a launch template). Each module must enforce input constraints using validation blocks, expose a complete set of typed outputs, and include a versions.tf file that pins the required Terraform version and AWS provider version. No module may contain hard-coded values; every configurable attribute must be an input variable with a description and type constraint.

The module library must live under a modules/ directory. A consumer configuration under environments/dev/ must call both modules, wire the networking module's outputs into the compute module's inputs, and produce a terraform plan that exits with code 0 using the hashicorp/aws provider pointed at a local mock endpoint (LocalStack or a null provider â your choice of mock strategy).

Acceptance Criteria

Running terraform validate inside environments/dev/ exits with code 0 and prints Success! The configuration is valid. â no warnings, no errors.
Every input variable in both modules has a type, a description, and at least one validation block; running terraform plan with a deliberately invalid input value (for example, a VPC CIDR that is not a valid CIDR notation) causes Terraform to exit with a non-zero code and print the custom error_message from the validation block.
Task 3: Automate the Terraform Lifecycle with an Orchestration Script
Problem Statement
Design and implement a single Bash orchestration script named tf-orchestrate.sh that accepts one positional argument â the target environment name (dev, staging, or prod) â and executes the full Terraform lifecycle for that environment in the correct order: tool-version check, init, validate, plan (saving the plan to a binary file named <environment>.tfplan), and a gated apply that only proceeds if the plan file exists and the user explicitly confirms. The script must handle errors at every stage: if any Terraform command exits with a non-zero code, the script must print a descriptive message identifying which stage failed and exit with a non-zero code itself without continuing to the next stage.

The script must also support a destroy sub-command (./tf-orchestrate.sh dev destroy) that runs terraform destroy with -auto-approve only after printing a ten-second countdown warning that can be interrupted with Ctrl-C.

Acceptance Criteria

Running ./tf-orchestrate.sh dev against the environments/dev/ configuration from Task 2 completes the init-validate-plan sequence, writes dev.tfplan to disk, prompts for confirmation before apply, and exits with code 0 when the user confirms; if any intermediate stage fails, the script exits immediately with a non-zero code and a message that names the failed stage (verified by temporarily introducing a syntax error into environments/dev/main.tf and confirming the script stops at the validate stage).
Running ./tf-orchestrate.sh dev destroy prints a visible ten-second countdown to stderr, can be cancelled with Ctrl-C before the countdown ends, and â if not cancelled â executes terraform destroy -auto-approve and exits with code 0; the script must refuse to run at all if called with an environment name other than dev, staging, or prod, printing a usage message and exiting with code 1.
Expected Outcomes
A version-controlled repository containing a two-module Terraform library, two environment configurations, and a fully error-handled orchestration script that manages the complete infrastructure lifecycle without manual Terraform commands.
Both acceptance criteria in Task 2 and both acceptance criteria in Task 3 pass demonstrably: terraform validate succeeds, invalid inputs produce custom error messages, the orchestration script gates apply behind confirmation, and the destroy countdown is interruptible.
Conclusion
This lab required you to make architectural decisions â module boundaries, variable contracts, output shapes, and error-handling strategy â without prescribed steps, which mirrors real infrastructure engineering work. The orchestration script pattern you built is the foundation of CI/CD pipeline integration for Terraform, where the same init-validate-plan-apply sequence runs unattended with human approval gates. Extend this foundation by adding remote state backends and policy-as-code checks as your next self-directed challenge.
